opportunities. The people who are really benefiting from affirmative action are the people in the jobs right now, like understanding that and accepting that as a possibility. I mean, these are things that I think are going to be really tough, and time will tell if people are really good, are willing to go there after the energy of just the outrage over the deaths of black people at the hands of police passes. The U.S. Census Bureau has projected that America's white population will drop below 50% in 2045 and that the nation will reach majority minority status. In your view, will that have any impact on the notion of white privilege? That's a good question. Well, those projections already do, right?
还是这只是人们为了感觉良好而做的事情？这是一个好问题。我怎么看？我认为对于有色人种来说，这在象征意义上很重要。这在人们对自己物理空间的舒适感方面很重要。我认为这种象征意义对白人来说本身也很重要。它改变了很多吗？我不这么认为。

20
00:14:30.000 --> 00:15:49.000
So there's research done by, say, Jennifer Richardson, an incredible social psychologist at Yale that shows that when you present people with that information, why it's often become threatened and respond in a way to try to maintain the privileges they have. When that actually comes to pass, I think if we haven't rectified the deep inequalities in the society right now, we're going to be in a really tough situation. where you will have the vast majority of the political and economic power in the hands of a minority. And it's not as if that doesn't exist in other places, but I think that is a dangerous place to be. And I think it's possible. There's nothing that suggests that simply the shift in the demographics of the country will necessarily eliminate the privileges associated with race. I anticipate people will try to hold on to their advantages and maybe even more tightly as they start to see them challenged. Is the tide toward pulling down Confederate monuments and renaming places that were named for Confederate generals, is that helpful in the scheme of things?
但我不知道我会不会把它仅仅贴上“感觉良好”的标签。我认为象征意义可以很重要，而且是有意义的。而且我认为拆除我所说的“白人至上”的偶像，因为很多纪念碑是在内战几十年后作为重申白人至上的一种方式而竖立起来的，拆除它们是象征性的，而且很重要。但这不会创造种族平等。美国心理学会刚刚发布了一项民意调查，发现绝大多数美国人，

21
00:15:49.000 --> 00:16:21.000
Or is that just something that people are doing to feel good? It's a good question. What do I think about that? I think that for people of color, it's important symbolically. It's important in terms of people's sense of comfort in their own physical space. I think that that symbolism for white folks is also important by itself. Does it change much? I don't think so.
72%的人认为我们的国家处于他们所观察到的最低点。调查还发现，71%的人表示针对少数族裔的警察暴力是他们感到压力的重要来源。与此同时，超过三分之二的人表示，目前反对系统性种族主义和警察暴行的运动将导致美国的有意义的变革。鉴于你所做的关于白人特权和种族主义的工作，你对这些数字有多大的信任度？我的意思是，我是否认真对待人们对国家现状的担忧？以及他们是否真的认为这会导致有意义的变革？我认为人们现在很担心。

22
00:16:21.000 --> 00:17:00.000
But I don't know that I would label it. but just a feel-good action. I think there's the symbolism can be important and is meaningful. And I think that pulling down icons of what I would call white superiority, because a lot of those monuments will put up much after the, you know, decades after the Civil War as a way of reasserting white superiority, pulling that down is symbolic and important. But that's not going to create racial equity. The American Psychological Association just released a poll that found a large majority of Americans,
我认为当你看到系统中的破坏时，这会引起合理的担忧。我认为人们确实相信事情会改变。我认为在某个时候很可能会宣布胜利。我有多认真地认为这种胜利会是真实的？这很难说。我对此不太乐观。所以我相信人们报告他们现在所经历的数字是诚实的。我认为这个国家旨在维持现状的机制极其强大。而且我认为人们可以从事维持现状的行为，并且在这样做时感到道德。我认为谁不想照顾自己的孩子，谁不想给自己的孩子他们能给的所有优势。

23
00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:41.000
72% believe our country is at the lowest point they have ever observed. It also found that 71% said police violence toward minorities is a significant source of stress that they're feeling. At the same time, just over two-thirds said the current movement against systemic racism and police brutality is going to lead to meaningful change in America. Given the work that you've done, looking at white privilege and racism, how much credence do you give to those numbers? And meaning do I take seriously that people are concerned about the state of the country right now? And that they really feel that this is going to lead to meaningful change? I think that people are concerned right now.
如果我们只是这样做，我们就会维持现状。所以我认为彻底改变这个国家的种族等级制度所需的工作量是巨大的。虽然我对抗议和示威感到鼓舞，但我对我们还需要走多远的感觉是，我所拥有的任何乐观情绪现在都极其谨慎。你计划在白人特权这个话题上进行什么进一步的研究？我们还需要了解什么？我认为我们需要了解很多。现在，我的研究正在发生一点变化。

24
00:17:41.000 --> 00:18:41.000
I think when you see the disruption in systems that causes legitimate concern. I think that people are, I think that people really do believe things. are going to change. I think that there's likely to be at some point the declaration of victory. How seriously do I think that victory will be real? That's harder to say. I'm less optimistic about that. So I believe the numbers in terms of people reporting, honestly, they've experienced right now. I think that the mechanisms in this country that are designed to maintain the status quo are incredibly powerful. And I think that people can engage in behaviors that maintain the status quo and actually feel moral while doing it. I think who doesn't want to take care of their child, who doesn't want to give their child all the advantages they can.
所以我对存在主义的担忧，我们如何理解自己以及我们如何在群体中进行社会理解，如何驱动我们的行为非常感兴趣。这包括群体间行为，身为黑人或白人意味着什么，身为美国人意味着什么，这如何驱动或创造我们的自我意识以及嫉妒地保护那个自我的愿望，这种基本的生存需求是如何驱动我们许多行为的。

25
00:18:41.000 --> 00:19:26.000
And if we just do that, we'll maintain the status quo. So I think that the amount of work it will take to radically shift the racial hierarchy in this country is vast. And while I am heartened by the demonstrations and protests, my sense of how much further we would need to go is such that any optimism, any optimizations, any optimism I have is incredibly guarded right now. What further research are you planning on the topic of white privilege? What do we still need to learn? I think we need to learn quite a bit. Right now, my research is moving a little bit.
这就是我现在的状态。我有许多学生继续研究白人特权，但老实说，我的兴趣现在更多地转向了我们深层的生存需求是如何驱动我们的群体间行为的。这个话题在学生中反响如何？你在课堂上谈论它时会遇到很多阻力吗？这是一个好问题。老实说，我并不经常在课堂上谈论这个。

26
00:19:26.000 --> 00:20:01.000
So I'm really interested in how existential concerns, how we understand ourselves and how we understand ourselves socially in groups drives our behavior. and that includes the inner group behavior, what it means to be a black person or a white person, what it means to be American, how that drives our or creates our sense of self and a desire to jealously guard that self, how that basic existential need is driving many of our behaviors.
所以我大部分时间在课堂上做的是领导力方面的工作。现在作为高级副院长，我在课堂上的时间更少了。所以我确实经常站在学生面前谈论他们的责任。在斯坦福工作，我们的学生既非常有才华，也非常有特权，正如我和所有与斯坦福作为学术机构以及许多其他真正精英和高地位学术机构相关的人一样。在这些群体面前，我说的是，当我们表现时，我们做出的决定会影响许多其他人。而且很容易专注于我们的决定如何影响我们的结果，它对我们的职业意味着什么，对我们的家庭意味着什么，而忽略了一个事实，即从我们的位置来看，这些决定实际上影响了许多人的生活。所以我并不特别谈论种族，但我试图给处于权力地位的人留下深刻印象的是，如果我们忽略了我们的行为对他人的后果，我们很可能会最终支持现有的不平等，对吧？加剧现有的不平等。这不需要我们成为坏人或种族主义者。它只需要我们继续顺应现状，或者继续试图最大化我们自己的结果。我认为我们有责任做更多的事情。

